#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923 :topic/id #uuid "1ed8dd8d-e728-5e57-81fe-a7c1a6a08469" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124481 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124294 :question/id #uuid "35290ca2-1488-52ba-9d2e-b8c3e04c45e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4743" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that $12=4 \\cdot 3,$ where $4$ is a perfect square. So, applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{12} =\\sqrt{4 \\cdot 3}= \\sqrt{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{3} = 2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "202575c6-7672-5387-a5ae-762bef520add" :knowledge-point/title "Separating a Square Root Into a Product of Two Square Roots"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124482 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124295 :question/id #uuid "204328e4-810f-591e-a085-bb9cdadca875" :question/math-academy-id "e-4744" :question/problem "Write $3 \\sqrt{5}$ as the square root of a single number." :question/worked-solution "Note that $3 = \\sqrt{9}.$ So, applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\sqrt{5} = \\sqrt{9}\\cdot\\sqrt{5}= \\sqrt{9\\cdot 5} = \\sqrt{45}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6f8c2c9-e82f-5d37-9f34-4811dd88c78d" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Product of Two Square Roots as a Single Square Root"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124483 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124296 :question/id #uuid "0c797a57-00a8-57b9-af30-655e8952c4c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-4756" :question/problem "Express $\\sqrt[3]{2}\\cdot\\sqrt[3]{3}$ as the cube root of a single number." :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{2}\\cdot\\sqrt[3]{3}= \\sqrt[3]{2\\cdot 3} = \\sqrt[3]{6}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97098b0-a7f9-564a-899e-60fb88682c80" :knowledge-point/title "The Product Rule With Cube Roots"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1927 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050496}] :topic/title "The Product Rule for Radicals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050848 :topic/id #uuid "6b88d999-b59b-5a39-b090-40b21de4e2a4" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184141 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184051 :question/id #uuid "d30aa8b4-820f-5ed1-8878-c5566805e3cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3491" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\sin^2\\left(150^\\circ\\right) + 2\\csc^2\\left(240^\\circ\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $150^\\circ$. From the unit circle, we see that the angle $150^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).\n$$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin 150^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n- Now, let's consider the angle $240^\\circ$. From the unit circle, we see that the angle $240^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right).\n$$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin 240^\\circ = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\csc{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc 240^{∘} &= \\frac{1}{\\sin 240^{∘}} \\\\\n&= (1)/\\left(\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} (150^{∘}) + 2\\csc^{2} (240^{∘}) &= \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + 2\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{8}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{35}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9355cf8a-c924-5d4b-a4c3-fe539bf34faf" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Involving Special Angles in Degrees"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184205 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184106 :question/id #uuid "b0506d9d-2495-505f-b61f-6c01607ad43f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9815" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle 4\\cos^2 \\left(\\dfrac \\pi 6\\right) - \\sin \\left(\\dfrac \\pi 4\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$. From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).\n$$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\cos\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {6}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\dfrac \\pi 4$. From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\dfrac \\pi 4$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right).\n$$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\sin\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\cos^{2} \\left(\\frac{π}{6}\\right) - \\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= 3 - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "298d3ee7-bb43-5835-8dc5-3306d1f9841f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Involving Special Angles in Radians"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3766 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050938}] :topic/title "Evaluating Trigonometric Expressions"}]}
